---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro'
import ledger from '../../data/shortlinks.json'

/*
 * The published map.
 *
 * A short link is a URL that hides where it goes, and on a site whose argument is
 * that a claim carries its provenance that is the wrong default. Three things fix
 * it and this page is the second: the code is the document's own section number
 * rather than a random string, the whole mapping is published here, and the
 * redirect is a 301 to a real page so the address bar shows the truth a moment
 * later. check-short.mjs fails the build if this page is missing a code.
 */
const entries = Object.entries(ledger as Record<string, { section: string; target: string; since: string }>)
const papers = await getCollection('papers')
const notes = await getCollection('impl')
const titles = new Map<string, string>()
for (const x of [...papers, ...notes]) titles.set(x.data.section, x.data.title)

const rows = entries.map(([code, e]) => ({ code, ...e, title: titles.get(e.section) ?? '' }))
const issued = rows.map((r) => r.since).sort()
---
<Doc title="Short links" description="Every short link this site has issued, what it points at, and the date it was issued.">
  <div class="mast">
    <div>Off the Bit
<b>Short links</b>
Codes: {rows.length}
Scheme: /l/ + section number</div>
    <div class="r"><b>M. Imran</b>
First issued {issued[0]}
Last issued {issued[issued.length - 1]}
Permanent</div>
  </div>

  <div class="title">
    <h1>Short links</h1>
    <div class="sub">Every code this site has issued, what it points at, and when it was handed out.</div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How the code is made</h2>
  <div class="ind prose">
    <p>A short link is the document's own section number with the dot replaced by a
    hyphen. Paper 5.14 is <code>/l/5-14</code>. Implementation note 3.2 is
    <code>/l/3-2</code>. Nothing is hashed and nothing is random, so a link tells you
    where it goes before you follow it, and this page lets you check that rather than
    take it on trust.</p>
    <p>The hyphen is not a decision made here. This document has written section
    numbers that way since the errata page was built, because a dot in a path reads as
    a file extension and gets swallowed when a URL ends a sentence.</p>
    <p>Both serving hosts answer the same codes.
    <code>imran.com.bd/l/5-14</code> is the same document as
    <code>mosthofaimran.com/l/5-14</code>, and neither redirects to the other: the
    canonical tag on the page does that work, which is the arrangement Section 2 of
    the index already describes.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="permanent">What a code promises</h2>
  <div class="ind prose">
    <p>A code is issued once and never moved. Not renamed, not repointed at a
    different document, not withdrawn if a paper is retracted: a retracted paper keeps
    its page, struck through, with the reason attached, and its short link keeps
    landing there. The build fails if any code in this table changes what it points
    at, which is the only reason a permanent redirect is safe to print on paper.</p>
    <p>Nothing is counted. Following one of these tells this site nothing, because
    there is nothing at the other end but a redirect written into a static file.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="map">The map</h2>
  <div class="ind">
    <table class="rt">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:74px">Code</th><th>Document</th><th style="width:104px">Issued</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="n" data-l="Code"><a href={`/l/${r.code}`}>{r.code}</a></td>
          <td data-l="Document"><a href={r.target}>{r.section} {r.title}</a></td>
          <td class="hd n" data-l="Issued">{r.since}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="foot"><span>Imran</span><span>Short links</span><span>{rows.length} codes</span></div>
</Doc>
